.TH _PRINTF 3 "2025" "Custom Library" "User Manual"

.SH NAME
_printf - custom formatted output conversion function

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include "main.h"
.PP
.B int _printf(const char *format, ...);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B _printf()
function produces output according to a
.I format
string. It is a simplified custom implementation of the standard C
.B printf(3)
function.

.PP
The function processes the format string one character at a time:
.IP \(bu 2
Ordinary characters are written directly to standard output.
.IP \(bu 2
A '%' character introduces a conversion specification used to format one
or more additional arguments provided after the format string.

.PP
Unlike the standard
.B printf(),
this implementation supports only a limited subset of conversion
specifiers and does not implement flags, field width, precision, or length
modifiers. The goal is to demonstrate understanding of variable argument
lists, memory handling, recursive printing, and low-level output.

.SH ARCHITECTURE OVERVIEW
The project is typically divided into the following components:

.TP
.B Format parser
Reads the format string and identifies conversion specifiers.

.TP
.B Dispatcher
Maps conversion specifiers to the correct handler function.

.TP
.B Printing helpers
Low-level functions that print characters, numbers, strings, binary
representations, and pointer addresses.

.TP
.B Output function
Wraps the use of the
.B write(2)
system call, ensuring all output is written to file descriptor 1 (stdout).

.SH SUPPORTED CONVERSION SPECIFIERS

.TP
.B %c
Prints a single character.

.TP
.B %s
Prints a null-terminated string.  
If a NULL pointer is passed, the string "(null)" is printed.

.TP
.B %d, %i
Prints a signed decimal integer.  
Handles:
.IP \(bu 2
negative values  
.IP \(bu 2
the limits of 32-bit integers  
.IP \(bu 2
recursive printing for multi-digit values

.TP
.B %b
Prints an unsigned integer in binary representation.

.TP
.B %p
Prints a memory address in hexadecimal pointer format.  
Typically prefixed with "0x".  
If NULL is passed, prints "(nil)".

.TP
.B %%
Prints a literal percent sign.

.SH UNSUPPORTED FEATURES
⚠️ The following are intentionally *not supported*:

.IP \(bu 2
Flags (-, +, space, #, 0)
.IP \(bu 2
Precision handling (.3f, .10s, etc.)
.IP \(bu 2
Width specifications (e.g., %8d)
.IP \(bu 2
Length modifiers (h, l, ll)
.IP \(bu 2
Floating-point formatting (%f, %g, %e)
.IP \(bu 2
Buffering and complex I/O
.IP \(bu 2
Locale-aware formatting

.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.B _printf()
returns the total number of characters printed (excluding the terminating
null byte).

On failure (for example, if the format string is NULL), the function
returns -1.

.SH ERROR HANDLING
Errors may occur under the following conditions:

.IP \(bu 2
The format string is NULL.  
.IP \(bu 2
An unsupported specifier follows a '%' character.  
.IP \(bu 2
Memory allocation fails (for pointer conversion).  
.IP \(bu 2
The write system call returns an error.

When an invalid specifier is encountered, the function prints the '%' and
the invalid character unchanged, preserving behavior similar to the
standard printf on some systems.

.SH EXAMPLE USAGE

.PP
.nf
#include "main.h"

int main(void)
{
    int len;

    len = _printf("Hello, %s!\\n", "Raghad");
    _printf("Printed %d characters.\\n", len);

    _printf("Char: %c, Number: %i\\n", 'A', 1234);
    _printf("Binary: %b\\n", 15);
    _printf("Pointer: %p\\n", (void *)0x1234abcd);
    _printf("Percent: %%\\n");

    return (0);
}
.fi

.SH INTERNAL FUNCTIONS
The following helper functions may be used:

.IP \(bu 2
_putchar  
.IP \(bu 2
print_string  
.IP \(bu 2
print_number  
.IP \(bu 2
print_binary  
.IP \(bu 2
print_pointer  
.IP \(bu 2
write (system call)  

.SH PERMITTED LIBRARIES
This project allows the following C library features:

.IP \(bu 2
stdarg.h – va_start, va_end, va_arg, va_copy  
.IP \(bu 2
unistd.h – write  
.IP \(bu 2
stdlib.h – malloc, free  
.IP \(bu 2
stdint.h – integer types  

.SH IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
Printing integers is typically implemented using recursion.  
.IP \(bu 2
Pointer conversion is implemented using manual hexadecimal conversion.  
.IP \(bu 2
Binary conversion repeatedly divides the number by 2.  
.IP \(bu 2
The function avoids using buffers unless required.  
.IP \(bu 2
The output is unbuffered and written directly to stdout.  

.SH BUGS
This implementation does not fully match the behavior of the standard
library
.B printf(3),
especially with formatting rules, width/precision, and undefined cases.

.SH SEE ALSO
printf(3), fwrite(3), stdarg(3), write(2)

.SH AUTHORS
Raghad Almalki / Mousa Alqarni
